  "query_preview": "../Starting Thursday/...The start of the long term \nforecast has trended drier as models have begun to agree that a \nlow pressure system will move south of the panhandle on Friday. \nThere maybe a few lingering periods of rain on Friday, but this \nwill be a well break from rain due to the multiple systems this \ncurrent week. Wind will be a different story as this low to the \nsouth will start to increase the pressure gradient between the \nCanadian border and SE AK. This will increase northerly winds \nacross the area. This northerly flow will allow a few northern \nareas to start to see temperatures fall below freezing. This \nleaves a low chance of Skagway and Haines seeing its first snow \nafter these northerly winds. Although confidence remains on the \nlow side. \n\nOtherwise active weather continues as deep low pressure systems \ncontinue to move into the gulf, then either near/into SE AK, or just \nsouth of SE AK. Whatever track these lows take will greatly \ndetermine what weather we see here on the panhandle. If these lows \ncontinue to move further south, we will continue to see more of a \nnortherly flow with the potential of colder air entering the area. \nIf these lows track further into the central or northern gulf, it \nwill bring more precipitation, while also continuing to have periods \nof stronger winds.",
